-- ══ DIVERGENCES, PLAINLY ═══════════════════════════════════════════

-- X1 E4 sid: a pre-registered BRANCH RESOLVED, not a failure. PRIMARY pinned
--    (sid conflicts 0, total 78 not 117, entities 503,404 not 503,443). The
--    §5.3 three-id-paths skew that motivated the alternative (distinct sids
--    503,372 < distinct ids 503,404) is REAL but does not touch the 39 pairs:
--    they agree on sid, so the skew lives elsewhere in the base. NOT MEASURED:
--    where. Recorded as a stage-2 question, not silently closed.
-- X2 PREDICATE COUNT IS 134, NOT THE 141 CITED THREE TIMES IN THE PRE FILE
--    (lines 102, 246, 333). The pre-registration's prose was wrong; the run is
--    right. RECONCILED EXACTLY, ZERO RESIDUAL, this round:
--      /tmp/rofl-measure/preds.py (1,944 B, present — an earlier claim in this
--      session that it was absent was FACTUALLY WRONG and is retracted here)
--      measures 60 node types / 48 edge types / 72 node meta keys / 17 edge
--      meta keys, and its own formula 4 core + 48 + 72 + 17 = 141.
--      Independently: node-meta ∪ edge-meta = 84, node-meta ∩ edge-meta = 5
--      (constructor, kind, method, path, source) — 72 + 17 double-counts those
--      five. 141 − 5 (double-counted) − 4 (line/column/endLine/endColumn
--      collapsed) + 1 (span/5) + 1 (conflict/5) = 134.
--      Manifest catalog kinds confirm it independently: core 4 + edge 48 +
--      meta 77 + retyped 3 + span 1 + conflict 1 = 134, and the 81 meta-like
--      predicates expand back to exactly the 84-key union with BOTH set
--      differences EMPTY. Third, independent cross-check: the manifest's five
--      `subject_universe == "node|edge"` predicates are EXACTLY the five
--      intersection keys.
--      NOTE: `node_type` values (60) are NOT predicates in variant A — types
--      are the VALUE of type/2, not predicate names. That is why 60 never
--      enters the arithmetic.
-- X3 DEV-P6-1 SHIPPED WEAKER THAN REGISTERED, AND WAS REPAIRED IN-ROUND. The
--    pre file registers «winner value+author+tick must agree»; b63a8049
--    compared the VALUE only, over the converter's own conflict subjects.
--    cec8a029 makes it four agreements over the UNION with the INPUT's
--    multi-record subjects, plus a mechanical coverage gate. The original check
--    DID pass on all 39; the repair is that it now COULD fail.
-- X4 MANIFEST PROVENANCE CARRIES NO GIT SHA. F3 registers «converter git SHA».
--    `ROFL_CONVERT_GIT_SHA` is read at COMPILE time and no build.rs bakes it
--    (build.rs is outside the E10 change envelope and would put git state into
--    every build of the whole crate). The honest default now names the failure
--    rather than a commit that was never captured:
--    `rfdb-0.4.1 git:not-baked(ROFL_CONVERT_GIT_SHA unset at compile time)`.
--    F3 is therefore PARTIALLY MET: input path, input sha, input size and input
--    manifest version are all present and verified; the converter revision is
--    identified by the reproducibility pair (input_recursive_sha256, this
--    store's own bytes) instead. Carried to stage 2 as an open item.
-- X5 canonical_state_sha's u32 author component is the RANK, not the NAME —
--    exactly as D6 pinned it, and §9.2's ban on interned ids in canonical
--    artifacts is honoured for predicate and perspective but NOT for author.
--    This is a real non-injectivity across stores. NOT changed (D6 is
--    pre-registered and immutable); ESCALATED as OWNER-RULINGS OQ-C3-1, stated
--    verbatim in the manifest's own `schemes.author_interning`, and PINNED by
--    `canonical_state_sha_author_component_is_the_rank_not_the_name` so no
--    future round can move it by accident.

-- ══ COMPILE-PHASE SEMANTIC FIXES (C-numbers) ═══════════════════════

-- C23 (facts/lsm.rs:332-350) — the round's ONLY compile-phase semantic call.
--   Ambiguity: D1 requires reading the old base THROUGH `LsmFactStore::open`
--   and S1 requires enumeration over ONE pinned snapshot, but the only two
--   accessors reaching that snapshot (`read_snapshot_of`, `store_read`) were
--   `#[cfg(test)] pub(crate)` test seams — they do not exist in a non-test
--   build. These produced the ONLY two compile errors in the 3,283-line
--   converter (E0599 at convert/mod.rs:361 and :363). Meanwhile E10's file list
--   excludes lsm.rs entirely.
--   Choice: un-gate the two accessors (drop `#[cfg(test)]`, retitle «Test seam»
--   → «Crate seam» naming the converter as the second consumer). Bodies
--   byte-identical, visibility stays pub(crate), no new public API, +7/−5.
--   Rationale: the alternative (open ManifestStore + MultiShardStore directly
--   inside convert/) was a REAL behaviour change against the pre-registration —
--   it would double-open the 543 MB base, contradicting D1's single read-only
--   open, and move S1's pin off the LsmFactStore snapshot. The minimal fix that
--   preserves pre-registered SEMANTICS beats the one that preserves the file
--   LIST. E10's operative criterion (storage_v2/, derive/plan*, derive/exec*,
--   graph/ diff EMPTY) was measured and IS empty; the crate's own hygiene test
--   `no_fact_store_consumer_outside_facts` mechanically proves facts/lsm.rs has
--   no consumer outside src/facts/, so it is provably off the engine hot path.
--   Flagged loudly (diff_scope_clean=false), never hidden.

-- ══ DEFECTS THE UNIT TESTS CAUGHT ══════════════════════════════════

-- T1 The blocker (finding 14) was reproduced BEFORE it was fixed:
--   `every_corrupted_length_is_a_typed_error_never_a_panic` walks every length
--   field of a real encoded segment, corrupts each in turn, and asserts a typed
--   `SegmentError`. On pre-fix code it PANICKED (slice index out of range on a
--   36-byte segment with a corrupted varint) — the test failing first is what
--   proved the reviewer's claim rather than assuming it.
-- T2 `c1_rejects_a_store_that_dropped_its_conflicts` is a falsification test,
--   not an assertion: it FIRST asserts the control store is non-vacuous
--   (input_multi_record_subjects 1, input_type_divergent_subjects 1,
--   type_conflict_subjects 1, consistent true), THEN strips the conflict facts
--   and requires resolved_view_consistent == false. On pre-fix code the
--   stripped store passed — which is precisely findings 21/22.
-- T3 The pre-existing tamper test had to be STRENGTHENED to survive the new
--   sha256 verification: it now RE-SEALS the manifest descriptor after
--   re-encoding the tampered row. That made it a better test — C1 must now
--   catch a wrong value in an INTERNALLY CONSISTENT store, which is exactly the
--   shape a converter bug produces (a real bug writes a consistent digest of
--   wrong bytes).
-- T4 `r1a_f3_numeric_author_min_agrees_with_shortlex_name_min` uses a
--   vocabulary where shortlex ≠ plain lexicographic (sorted: Z, b, aa, $legacy)
--   and pins that F3-over-ids picks the shortlex-least NAME. It also asserts
--   the REAL converter's author table is strict shortlex — the invariant the
--   equivalence rests on, which had zero coverage before (finding 25).
-- NOT RECORDED by the implementation phase: whether any of ITS 21 tests caught
--   a defect during development. The phase reported only the final green run,
--   so this ledger cannot claim otherwise.

-- ══ REVIEW FINDINGS — ALL 35, WITH DISPOSITION ═════════════════════
-- Severities as filed: 1 blocker, 10 major, 14 minor, 10 note. Five duplicate
-- pairs: 21≡29, 10≡30, 17≡31, 2≡33, 20≡35 — each fixed once, both listed.

-- 14 blocker read_segment panics on a corrupted varint length — FIXED.
--    Bounded `Cursor` (`remaining`/`take`/`bounded_len`/`bounded_count`),
--    varint overflow rejected. Test T1. The reviewer was right and the panic
--    was reproduced before the fix, not argued about.
-- 15 major unbounded Vec::with_capacity from attacker-controlled row_count /
--    string_count (703 GiB reserved from a ~40 B file) — FIXED: both counts are
--    clamped against the bytes actually remaining before any allocation.
-- 1  major five silent-drop counters ungated and invisible to C1 — FIXED:
--    `gate_silent_drops` (convert/mod.rs) turns all five into a typed
--    E-CONV-ENVELOPE abort. Test `every_silent_drop_counter_aborts_the_conversion`
--    (all five abort; an EXACT duplicate still converts — the distinction
--    matters, exact duplicates lose nothing).
-- 2  major (≡33) verify_c1 normalises COLUMN_META_KEYS away on the INPUT side,
--    so S5 can never fail C1 — FIXED via option (1) of the reviewer's own two:
--    `blob_copies_differing > 0` is now an abort, which makes the reader-side
--    filter SAFE because the differing case can no longer reach it. Test
--    `a_blob_copy_that_disagrees_with_its_column_aborts_the_run` (agreeing
--    control converts; differing aborts). Why not option (2) ($meta: emission):
--    it would mint a NEW predicate on data no measured base carries, i.e. ship
--    an untested vocabulary path to handle a case we abort on anyway.
-- 17 major (≡31) load_run never verified the sha256 it wrote, nor arity —
--    FIXED: bytes are read ONCE, length drift rejected, sha256 verified against
--    THOSE bytes, then `decode_segment` (split out of `read_segment` precisely
--    so verify and decode cannot see different bytes) decodes the same buffer,
--    then arity is checked against the catalog. Test
--    `a_segment_whose_bytes_disagree_with_the_manifest_digest_is_rejected`.
--    CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION on the real base: every run now verifies all 2,776
--    digests, which had NEVER been checked before this round.
-- 16 major Q2 catalog cannot reconstruct the semantics it pins — FIXED:
--    `DeclJson.columns` (span/5's endLine is no longer an untyped Int),
--    `DeclJson.subject_universe`, and `SchemesJson` recording canon / fid /
--    eid / routing / bloom / author_interning / state_sha as fixed strings of
--    this format version. They are recorded, not computed, so they cost nothing
--    and cannot drift silently.
-- 21 major (≡29) DEV-P6-1 is value-only and driven by the converter's own
--    conflicts, so it cannot see a MISSING conflict — FIXED, see X3.
-- 22 major no run-level gate fires if ZERO conflicts are emitted — FIXED:
--    the coverage gate plus verify_c1's INPUT-derived non-vacuity
--    (`type_divergent ⊆ conflict_subjects` AND
--    `exceptions.len() == type_losers.len()`), with the three new report fields
--    proving the derivation was non-empty (39/39/39). Test T2.
-- 8  major E7's gate object contains a caller-environment field — FIXED:
--    `--input` is canonicalized, and the fix is PROVEN, not argued: run f3 with
--    `/tmp/rofl-p2-real-base/./graph.rfdb` produced a byte-identical store with
--    an identical `provenance.input_path`. Residual (different mount point)
--    stated in E7 and in the code comment, not hidden.
-- 9  major canonical_state_sha digests the author RANK, not the NAME —
--    ACKNOWLEDGED, DOCUMENTED, PINNED, ESCALATED; not changed. See X5 and
--    OWNER-RULINGS OQ-C3-1. Rejecting the code change is a D6 (pre-registered,
--    immutable) constraint, NOT a disagreement with the reviewer: the collision
--    class is real and is now stated in the store's own manifest.
-- 3  minor content_hash absent from NormNode — FIXED: the field exists and C1
--    compares it; the doc states it can only ever be 0 BECAUSE the envelope
--    aborts otherwise, so anyone who relaxes that gate turns C1 red instead of
--    quietly making it blind.
-- 4  minor failed winner-fid lookup swallowed by unwrap_or_default — FIXED:
--    typed E-CONV-VERIFY naming the subject and the fid.
-- 5  minor (≡24) type_cross_checked_subjects reported the LOSER count — FIXED:
--    it is now `cross_checked_subjects.len()`, an actual subject count
