-- ══ STAGE-2 CONTRACT (what stage 1 actually learned) ═══════════════
--
-- S2-a ENGINE ROUTING (§5.2). Ship `blake3_u64(canon(subject)) % shard_count`
--   in the ENGINE write path. Stage 1 already IMPLEMENTED and PROVED this
--   function over 2,776 segments and recorded it in
--   `schemes.routing`, so stage 2 copies a verified rule instead of inventing
--   one. HARDER THAN EXPECTED: the reverse run routes by ITS OWN leading column,
--   so a fact's two runs live in DIFFERENT shards by construction — any
--   engine-side «one fact, one shard» assumption is already false in the data
--   on disk.
-- S2-b MVCC CONFLICT KEY. Replace `file_last_committed_version`
--   (storage_v2/multi_shard.rs:143) with `(perspective, predicate, subject)`.
--   Stage 1 makes this concrete: the converted store has no `file` dimension at
--   all, so the current key cannot even be computed over it.
-- S2-c OPEN SegmentType / ManifestStore. `storage_v2/types.rs from_u8` rejects
--   any third variant today — that is, in code, WHY the engine cannot read the
--   converted store. Stage 1 deliberately kept storage_v2 at a ZERO diff, so
--   this is an additive change against a frozen baseline. Stage 2 either adopts
--   the ROFLSEG1 header or re-encodes once; the format is now fully
--   self-describing (columns, subject_universe, schemes, per-segment sha256 +
--   arity, all verified on load), so a re-encoder can be written against the
--   manifest alone.
-- S2-d node_view §3.3 materialised Composite + the http_server.rs full-scan
--   consumers. EASIER: the four node_view components plus sid are already
--   Functional with the seeded author_priority on all five decls, the R-1a
--   resolution is already computed, and the 39 arbitrations are already
--   DURABLE as conflict/5 in `audit` — node_view can read the resolution
--   instead of recomputing it. HARDER: the losers are LIVE (Q10), so every
--   consumer that assumes «one live fact per (subject, Functional predicate)»
--   is wrong on 39 subjects TODAY and must go through the resolver.
-- S2-e LEGACY READ PATHS onto FactStore: cypher/, datalog/eval.rs
--   reverse_attr_lookup, graph view. Stage 1 pins the shape they must speak:
--   134 predicates, 65 node-subject / 51 pair / 12 edge / 5 node|edge / 1 fact
--   universes, span/5 as a Composite (four keys collapsed), edge metadata keyed
--   by a COMPUTED EID with no `edge_of/3` to join through. That last one is the
--   single biggest surprise for a legacy reader: to find an edge's metadata you
--   must recompute `fid("main", <EDGE_TYPE>, [Id(src), Id(dst)])`. It is
--   recorded in `schemes.eid`, but every consumer needs the helper.
-- S2-f GATES. C2 (six enrichers back on BatchHandle.commit), C6 (pack-phase
--   perf fence 56.2 / 74 s), C8 (crash_recovery rewritten on supersession —
--   §10.4 pins it to the P7 crash gate), C3-full (compaction ⊕-invariance),
--   C4 (P8 backends), C5 (v0 corpus 29 tests). Stage 1 contributes the
--   INSTRUMENT for C3-full: `canonical_state_sha` is computed by a reader over
--   written bytes, so a compaction that changes it has provably changed the
--   logical state — subject to OQ-C3-1 (the author-rank component) being ruled
--   on FIRST if the comparison is cross-backend.
-- S2-g WHAT STAGE 1 DISCOVERED THAT CHANGES THE PLAN:
--   (i) the 2.995x byte growth is dominated by a FIXED 35 B assertion column
--       per physical row (39.1% of the store). Stage 2's storage budget is
--       decided by the row shape, not by the tuple data — A′ RLE over the
--       assertion columns is the lever, and it is worth more than any value
--       encoding.
--   (ii) the per-SEGMENT string table stores a repeated string once per shard
--       (1,252,486 entries, 28.3% of the store). A store-wide dictionary is a
--       second, independent lever.
--   (iii) `metrics.entities` is a SUBJECT count, and the sid predicate is the
--       only carrier of that notion in the new layout — stage 2's node_view
--       must not treat live-fact counts as entity counts.
--   (iv) 4,997 JSON-composite metadata values survive as Term($json,[Str]).
--       Today's attr path DROPS them. Any stage-2 read path that reproduces
--       the old `json_scalar_to_string` behaviour would silently re-introduce
--       the loss C1 just proved is gone.
